TI  - DIETARY LINOLEATE AND BLOOD-PRESSURE
T2  - MILCHWISSENSCHAFT-MILK SCIENCE INTERNATIONAL
AB  - Healthy volunteers (100 males and 61 females) were at random assigned to 2 dietary groups. Group A (81 participants) was given a balanced diet allowing a daily intake of 6 g of linoleic acid, group B was allowed 45 g of linoleic acid per day. The groups were quite homogeneous in their professional and economic status, the average age of the males was 45 .+-. 9, that of the females 47 .+-. 8 yr. They had normal blood pressure, normal cardiac status and no detectable circulatory disturbances. Persons  receiving medication were not accepted. A series of various parameters was studied before the start of the experiments and subsequently, 6 times in weekly intervals. Blood samples were taken before breakfast after a 12 h fast. The patients were divided into normolipemic groups and those belonging to types II and IV according to Fredrickson classification; the diets were carefully planned, they were repeatedly analyzed and were readily accepted by the participants. They caused no ill effects. During the hospitalization 4 persons belonging to group A and 9 of group B were excluded leaving 148 cases for final evaluation. No circulatory episodes (infarctions, strokes or peripheral arterial occlusions) or other complications occurred. For the statistical evaluation of the data nonparametric tests were used because it could not be assumed that the groups were normally constituted. The U test of Wilcoxon, Mann and Whitney and the Chi-square test with Yates'' correlation (n > 20) or that of Fisher (n < 20) was used. No decrease of blood pressures after the intake of the linoleic acid rich diet was observed. This was true when all patients belonging to the 2 groups were studied or when subgroups were formed in which participants with the same classification according to Fredrickson were included. In group B, the linoleic acid content of blood lipids was elevated but total cholesterol and apolipoprotein B were reduced. In the persons of Fredrickson type II of group B, the decrease in total serum cholesterol and apolipoprotein B was particularly outspoken. For the 0.1-0.2% of the adult population suffering from familiar hypercholesterolemia and the 3-5% afflicted with secondary hypercholesterolemia a serum cholesterol depressing effect can be expected from the administration of large amounts of linoleate. The results do not justify the replacement of animal fats (butter) by vegetable fats for the  majority of the population.
