AU  - Charles, SM
AU  - Sanders, TAB
TI  - Impaired postprandial endothelial function depends on the type of fat consumed by healthy men
T2  - JOURNAL OF NUTRITION
AB  - Postprandial lipemia impairs endothelial function possibly via an oxidative stress mechanism A stearic acid-rich triacylglycerol (TAG) (shea butter) results in a blunted postprandiai increase in plasma TAG compared with an oleic acid-rich TAG; however, its acute effects or endothelial function and oxidative stress are unknown. A randomized crossover trial (n = 17 men) compared the effects of 50g fat, rich in stearic acid [shea butterblend (SA)] or oleic acid [high oleic sunflower oil 00)], on changes in endothelial function [brachial artery flow-mediated dilatation (FMD)], arterial tone [pulse wave analysis (PWA), and carotid-femoral pulse wave velocity (PWVc-f)], and oxidative stress (plasma 8-isoprostane F2 alpha) at fasting and 3 h following the test meals. The postprandial increase in plasma TAG was lower (66% lower incremental area under curve) following the SA meal [28.3 (9.7,46.9)) than after the HO meal (83.4 (57.0, 109.8); P < 0.001] (geometric means with 95% Cl, arbitary units). Following the HO meal, there was a decrease in FMD [-3.0% (-4.4, -1.6); P < 0.0011 and an increase in plasma 8-isoprostane F2 alpha (10.4ng/L (3.8, 16.9); P = 0.005] compared with fasting values, but no changes followed the SA meal, The changes in 8-isoprostane F2a and FMD differed between meals and were 14.0 ng/L (6.4, 21.6; P = 0.001) and 1.75% (0.10, 3,39; P = 0.02), respectively. The reductions in PWA and PWV c-f did not differ between meals. This study demonstrates that a stearic acid-rich fat attenuates the postprandial impairment in endothelial function compared with an oleic acid-rich fat and supports the hypothesis that postprandial lipemia impairs endothelial function via an increase in oxidative stress.
