TI  - Dietary habits and psychosocial factors as a risk for development of coronary heart disease in Bulgarian women
T2  - FRONTIERS IN CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE
CP  - 5th International Congress on Coronary Artery Disease
AB  - There are common risk factors for cardiovascular diseases in female and male sex but also some differences in their risk profile. Aim: to explore the diet of Bulgarian healthy women and their psychosocial status in conditions of economic transition. Material and methods Random population sample of 146 women free of cardiovascular diseases at mean age 58.14+/-8.75 years was studied. All participants were interviewed extensively for smoking and dietary habits, economic changes, the effect of stress and psychological factors, etc. To determine the type of personality a standard Framingham score was used. Results. Only 16% of the participants consume fish at least once a week. Fresh vegetables and fruits in winter arc limited to less than 10% of the sample. Yogurt and white cheese are common in the everyday menu - 68% and 65% respectively. Salt consumption is evenly distributed across the quartiles of systolic blood pressure. The financial status has worsened in 75% of the women. The everyday stress is considered moderate to high by 62% of the females. 70% has defined themselves as type A personality. Conclusions. The protective food products such as fish, fruits and vegetables are undervalued in the diet of the studied women, either due to financial constraints or because of acquired bad habits. The women in Bulgaria consider stress and financial difficulties important factors in their everyday life.
   Coronary heart disease (CHD) is a major cause of death in females worldwide (1), especially in the countries in economic transition, like is Bulgaria where cardiovascular diseases are responsible for 70 % of all deaths in women in 2001 (2). Most of the major risk factors for CHD like smoking, dyslipidemia, hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and others arc common for both sexes although the relative impact is gender specific. The "diet-heart" hypothesis has long been dominant by testing the relationships between saturated and polyunsaturated fats and cholesterol to risk of CHD (3,4). Some studies conducted in females has proved the effect of different types of nutrients and dietary products like saturated and unsaturated fat, trans fatty acids (5-7), fish and n-3 fatty acids (8-10), proteins (11), fiber (12,13), etc. Recently, dietary pattern analysis has been proposed as an alternative approach to examining the relationship between diet and risk of chronic diseases because it is more close to the real world where people cat meals with complex combinations of nutrients (14). During the last decade, a growing body of research has confirmed that psychosocial and socioeconomic factors are associated with heart disease risk and outcomes in women. Socioeconomic status, economic difficulties, hostility, stress, deprivation and lack of social support, depression, and type A personality are all studied in their relationship with CHD (15-17).
   The aim of the present study was to explore the dietary pattern by food-frequency questionnaire and the effect of some psychosocial factors on healthy women in a country in economic transition.
